    Bug 2062218 - Re-enable signon.storage.rust.enabled for Thunderbird. r=tobyp,jtracey

    The Rust backend normalizes every stored origin to origin-only form: paths are
    dropped, and opaque URLs lose everything after the scheme. That broke two places.

    For calendar:
     - Remove _ensureOrigin(), which was a compatibility fallback to be dropped
       after Lightning 6.2. It returned the full spec for opaque oauth: URLs, and
       otherwise truncated to prePath or prefixed a bare hostname with https://.
       Every caller already passes nsIURI.prePath, so passwordManagerSave/Get/Remove
       now use the origin they are given, and test_auth_utils.js loses the cases
       that only exercised those fallbacks.

     - The opaque oauth: case is the one the Rust backend can't keep: it normalizes
       stored origins to origin-only form, collapsing "oauth:{calendarId}" to
       "oauth:". Nothing has read those legacy Lightning logins since calendar OAuth
       moved to OAuth2Module ("oauth://{issuer}"), but they can still be sitting in
       a profile and migration rewrites their origin - so browser_calDAV_oAuth.js was
       updated to expect that.

    For nntp:
     - Fix a pre-existing bug the Rust backend exposed. MsgAuthPrompt stores a
       per-group password under the server origin (news://host[:port]) with the full
       group URI as the realm, but all three lookup sites searched with the full
       group URI as the origin, so they never matched. Search by prePath instead.
       The test only passed because it inserted logins in a form the product never writes.

    Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D321166

